Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.32,0:00:05.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the shoebox. Spread its\Nphotos out by their {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} reading.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.74,0:00:10.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The line to cross is zero:\Ndraw a thin strip around it.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.08,0:00:13.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each photo also needs a speedometer.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.72,0:00:16.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No clock required:\Nthe dipole's equation turns
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.97,0:00:20.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the photo's three dial\Nreadings into a speed.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.45,0:00:23.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Up or down, both count,\Nso keep only its size.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.72,0:00:26.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Add the speeds inside the strip,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.04,0:00:31.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then divide by its width and\Nby the total number of photos.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.61,0:00:33.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Out comes the {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.70,0:00:37.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Shrink the strip to nothing,\Nand for well-behaved
Dialogue: 0,0:00:37.25,0:00:40.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}systems the recipe is\Nexact: Rice's formula.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.74,0:00:44.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The catch: few photos land\Nin a thin strip. Here,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:44.28,0:00:47.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}about two dozen out of eight thousand.
